 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: The Bank of Scotland was founded by an Act of the Scottish Parliament in 1695, a year after the Bank of England. Three Scottish banks still print their own notes: the Bank of Scotland, the Royal Bank of Scotland and the Clydesdale Bank. They are sterling, worth exactly the same as a Bank of England note, though a shop down south may squint at one.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "econ_scottish_notes",
   "title": "Scottish Banknotes"
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "edinburgh_gododdin",
   "title": "Y Gododdin",
   "text": "Around the year 600 the Gododdin, a British people whose stronghold was Din Eidyn, here at Edinburgh, rode south to battle at Catraeth, probably Catterick in Yorkshire, and hardly any came back. The poem that mourns them, Y Gododdin, is one of the oldest in Welsh. So one of the oldest Welsh poems is about Edinburgh.",
   "people": "celts"
  },
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 46,
   "plaque": "edinburgh_wojtek",
   "title": "Wojtek the Soldier Bear",
   "text": "Wojtek was a Syrian brown bear cub adopted by soldiers of the Polish II Corps in Iran in 1942. Enlisted so he could travel with them, he is said to have carried ammunition at Monte Cassino in 1944. After the war his unit came to Scotland, and he lived at Edinburgh Zoo until 1963. His statue is in Princes Street Gardens.",
   "people": "eastern_european"
  },
  {
   "text": "THE FLORAL CLOCK. A working clock made from thousands of plants, laid out afresh every spring, and the oldest in the world. It keeps good time. It also keeps its hands, very tidily, in the border.",
   "x": 10.0,
   "y": 19.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE SCOTT MONUMENT. Sir Walter Scott's Gothic spire, 61 metres tall and the biggest monument to a writer anywhere. There are 287 steps to the top. Scott wrote about forty books. The steps are fewer than the pages.",
   "x": 25.0,
   "y": 16.0
  },
  {
   "text": "PRINCES STREET GARDENS. Once the Nor' Loch, a muddy lake under the Castle. It was drained, and the gardens were laid out in the early 1800s. It's far more peaceful now. The lake monsters left.",
   "x": 33.0,
   "y": 11.0
  },
  {
   "text": "EDINBURGH CASTLE. On Castle Rock, the stump of a volcano quiet for about 340 million years. The Honours of Scotland live inside. The One O'Clock Gun fires every day but Sunday. People set their watches by it.",
   "x": 14.0,
   "y": 42.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE ESPLANADE. A flat parade ground in front of the Castle, where the Royal Edinburgh Military Tattoo is held every August. A lot of pipers, drummers and dancers. A lot of tartan, too, worn in the proper way.",
   "x": 26.0,
   "y": 41.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE ROYAL MILE. About a mile of closes, wynds, shops and old stairs from the Castle to Holyrood. Underneath lie buried streets like Mary King's Close. A city that kept a very old diary.",
   "x": 35.0,
   "y": 40.0
  },
  {
   "text": "HOLYROOD. The King's home in Scotland. Mary, Queen of Scots, lived here, and so did a choir of very shiny crowns. At the foot of the Mile, it's the end of the road and the beginning of the park.",
   "x": 49.0,
   "y": 42.0
  },
  {
   "text": "AULD REEKIE. Edinburgh's old nickname, from the smoke of chimneys when the Old Town was packed in tight. It's far fresher now. The nickname stuck. Nicknames do.",
   "x": 36.0,
   "y": 33.0
  },
  {
   "text": "DÙN ÈIDEANN. The Gaelic name of this city, 'the fort of Eidyn'. It's pronounced about like 'doon AY-dinn'. Say it with a straight face and a few locals will take you aside to say it better.",
   "x": 10.0,
   "y": 27.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE GRASSMARKET. Once a cattle market under the Castle, now a square of pubs and shops. Greyfriars Bobby, the Skye terrier who guarded his master's grave for fourteen years, is a short walk away.",
   "x": 12.0,
   "y": 51.0
  },
  {
   "text": "ARTHUR'S SEAT. A hill made from an extinct volcano, 251 metres high, right beside the city. It's an easy hour's walk to the top, with views of the whole of Edinburgh, the Forth and, on a good day, a smug seagull who got there first.",
   "x": 57.0,
   "y": 49.0
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 39,
   "plaque": "edinburgh_heart_of_midlothian",
   "title": "Heart of Midlothian",
   "text": "Outside St Giles' a heart is set into the cobbles. It marks the doorway of the Old Tolbooth, the town jail, which was pulled down in 1817. Locals spit on it, for luck or out of contempt for the old prison, depending on who you ask. Please admire it from a dry distance."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 49,
   "plaque": "edinburgh_deacon_brodie",
   "title": "Deacon Brodie",
   "text": "William Brodie was a respected cabinetmaker and town councillor by day and a burglar by night, copying the keys of houses he worked in. He was hanged in 1788. Robert Louis Stevenson grew up with a cabinet Brodie had made, and later wrote Jekyll and Hyde. Mind who fits your locks."
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 44,
   "plaque": "edinburgh_adam_smith",
   "title": "Adam Smith",
   "text": "Adam Smith, author of The Wealth of Nations, spent his last years in the Canongate, the lower end of the Royal Mile, and is buried in the Canongate Kirkyard. The great champion of free trade worked here as a customs commissioner, collecting duties. Economists still find that very funny."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 39,
   "plaque": "edinburgh_dolly_the_sheep",
   "title": "Dolly the Sheep",
   "text": "Dolly, born in July 1996 at the Roslin Institute just outside Edinburgh, was the first mammal cloned from an adult cell. She was named after Dolly Parton and had six lambs of her own. She now stands, stuffed and very patient, in the National Museum of Scotland."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 3,
   "text": "NORTH TO THE FORTH. South Queensferry and the three Forth bridges, then the Kingdom of Fife: St Andrews and, over the Tay, Dundee."
  },
  {
   "text": "FINGERPOST: SOUTH to the SCOTTISH BORDERS: the Tweed valley, Melrose and the Eildon Hills. Pack a raincoat and a good reason.",
   "x": 17,
   "y": 60
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "THE ROSLIN INSTITUTE, Easter Bush, south of the city. Visitor room open to all. Part of the University of Edinburgh, home of animal genetics and one very famous sheep."
  },
  {
   "x": 60,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "edinburgh_maxwell",
   "title": "James Clerk Maxwell",
   "text": "James Clerk Maxwell was born at 14 India Street in Edinburgh's New Town in 1831. His equations of the 1860s showed that electricity, magnetism and light are one and the same, and predicted waves nobody had yet seen. Heinrich Hertz found them in the 1880s: radio. In 1861 Maxwell also showed the first colour photograph, of a tartan ribbon."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 7,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Arthur Conan Doyle, creator of Sherlock Holmes, was born near here at 11 Picardy Place on 22 May 1859.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "edinburgh_conan_doyle",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Arthur Conan Doyle"
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 41,
   "plaque": "arts_burns_edinburgh",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Burns in Edinburgh",
   "text": "In July 1786 a Kilmarnock printer ran off 612 copies of Poems, Chiefly in the Scottish Dialect by an Ayrshire farmer who was thinking of emigrating to Jamaica. By November Robert Burns was in Edinburgh instead, the talk of every drawing room, and a second edition came out the next spring. At a party that winter a fifteen-year-old Walter Scott was the only one in the room who knew whose lines were printed under a picture, and got a word of thanks for it, which he dined out on for the rest of his life. Burns found the gentry tiring and went home. The gentry have been claiming him ever since."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "arts_stevenson",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Robert Louis Stevenson",
   "text": "Robert Louis Stevenson was born in Edinburgh on 13 November 1850 and grew up at 17 Heriot Row, a sickly boy watching the lamplighter from the window. He wrote Treasure Island in 1883, and in 1886 both Kidnapped and Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde, a tale of a respectable man with a second self that owes something to this double city of Old Town and New. He died in Samoa in 1894, aged 44. 'Fifteen men on the dead man's chest' began here, in a wet New Town bedroom."
  }
 ],
